## Why This Is Asked

Production ML systems must adapt to concept drift and data skew without sacrificing stability. This question probes concrete, actionable strategies beyond theory.

## Key Concepts

- Concept drift detection
- Sliding window retraining
- Score calibration and monitoring
- Safe deployment and rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal drift check sketch (conceptual)
function detectDrift(scores, windowSize, threshold) {
  const w = scores.slice(-windowSize);
  const mean = w.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)/w.length;
  return Math.abs(mean - 0.5) > threshold;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the rollback path before production deployment?
- Which telemetry would you collect to prove drift handling works?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical lightweight baseline building for text routing under strict latency constraints, in a data-scarce regime with model calibration requirements. Also covers drift monitoring and retraining cadence.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight text representation with a fixed 30-feature vector
- SGD-based multiclass logistic regression or tiny decision tree for efficiency
- Calibration via temperature scaling
- Drift detection using rolling holdout validation
- Deployment latency and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Training loop outline for SGD multiclass LR
const model = new SGDLogisticRegression({
  learningRate: 0.01,
  epochs: 100,
  batchSize: 32
});
model.fit(XTrain, yTrain);
// Temperature scaling for calibration
const temp = calibrateTemperature(model, XVal, yVal);
// Inference
